  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "### Added\n\n- `ScanTpmKeys` and `ScanTpm` in `HardwareIdsConfig` (.NET and C++), both enabled by default. The TPM's public keys and\n  certificates take most of the time of a scan, a few hundred milliseconds against about 10 ms for everything else:\n  `ScanTpmKeys = false` leaves them out and keeps what TBS and the TPM capabilities report, `ScanTpm = false` leaves the\n  whole TPM out. In C, `HardwareIds_GetSnapshotJsonEx` takes the `HARDWAREIDS_SKIP_TPM_KEYS` and `HARDWAREIDS_SKIP_TPM`\n  flags; `HardwareIdsSnapshot` takes `--no-tpm-keys` and `--no-tpm`.\n\n### Changed\n\n- `GetHwidAsync` runs the collectors on the thread pool: awaiting it no longer blocks the calling thread while the\n  hardware and the TPM are read. `GetHwid` still runs them on the caller's thread.\n- The README packed for NuGet.org starts with a Markdown title in place of the HTML header, which NuGet.org printed\n  as text, and its relative links point to the files of the release on GitHub.\n- Every build compares the public API of the package with the previous release, and fails on a removed or changed member.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- The `$id` of `hwid.schema.json` names the release (`.../v2.5.0/hwid.schema.json`) instead of the `master` branch. A\n  validator that caches schemas by identifier, opis/json-schema for one, resolved the references of a second version\n  against the first one it had loaded; the schemas of 2.3.0 and 2.4.0 share an identifier but also the same definitions.\n- `Hwid.LibraryVersion` is `null` after deserializing a snapshot that has no `library_version` (any version before\n  2.4.0), instead of the version of the library that deserialized it. The property is a nullable string accordingly.",
